---
import SkillPage from '../../layouts/SkillPage.astro';
import skillSource from '../../data/calculus-tutor.skill.txt?raw';

const description =
  'A free Claude skill that tutors calculus one step at a time. It guides you to the answer instead of handing it over, and you reply by letter, so you never type math.';

// A study aid, not a developer tool, so the schema says EducationalApplication.
// featureList restates what SKILL.md does, in plain lines answer engines can lift.
const featureList = [
  'Reads the problem from a screenshot, a photo, or the web page you are viewing',
  'Guides one step at a time instead of giving the answer',
  'Offers lettered choices so the student never types math',
  'Builds the wrong choices from common mistakes',
  'Lets the student set the pace with hint, show this step, skip, or show me the rest',
  'Ends with a takeaway, a quick way to check the answer, and an optional practice problem',
];
---

<SkillPage
  slug="calculus-tutor"
  name="Calculus Tutor"
  description={description}
  ogImage="/og-calculus-tutor.png"
  standfirst="Share a calculus problem and it walks you through it one step at a time. You answer with a letter, so you never type math."
  sourceLabel="calculus-tutor / SKILL.md"
  source={skillSource}
  download="/downloads/calculus-tutor.skill"
  downloadBlurb="Works in Claude Code or a Claude app. Drop it in, then share a problem and ask for help with it."
  applicationCategory="EducationalApplication"
  featureList={featureList}
>
  <p>Ask a chatbot for help with a calculus problem and it usually solves the whole thing. The homework gets done and nobody learns anything. Ask it to tutor you instead and you hit a second wall: it wants you to type your work, and typing math into a chat box is slow and miserable.</p>
  <p>I wrote this skill to fix both. You share the problem as a screenshot, a photo, or the page you're already on. It tells you in plain words what the problem is asking, then gives you the first step as a nudge. When it needs an answer from you, it offers two to four lettered choices. The wrong choices come from common mistakes, so a wrong pick shows exactly what to explain. You set the pace. Say "hint", "show this step", "skip", or "show me the rest" and it does that.</p>
  <p>The whole thing is right here. Read it, copy it, or download it. If it gets you or a student you know through a problem set, tell me.</p>
</SkillPage>
